1️⃣ Why Async Processing Is Needed
Problem with synchronous code

Example: Banking system transferring money.

Account account = accountService.getAccount(id);
FraudCheck fraud = fraudService.check(account);
Notification.send(account);

Each call waits for the previous one.

If each call takes:

DB → 200 ms

Fraud API → 500 ms

Notification → 300 ms

Total = 1000 ms

Async Solution

Run independent tasks in parallel.

CompletableFuture<Account> accountFuture =
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> accountService.getAccount(id));

CompletableFuture<FraudCheck> fraudFuture =
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fraudService.check(id));

Now both run simultaneously.

Total time ≈ 500 ms instead of 1000 ms.
Asynchronous processing means tasks run without blocking the main thread, allowing other work to continue while waiting for slow operations (like I/O).

Key Reasons:

Better Performance ⚡

The system can process multiple tasks concurrently instead of waiting for each task to finish.

Example: Web servers handling thousands of requests simultaneously.

Improved User Experience 🙂

Applications stay responsive.

Example: In a mobile app, data loads in the background while the UI remains usable.

Efficient Resource Utilization 🧠

CPU doesn’t stay idle while waiting for slow operations like database queries, file reads, or API calls.

Scalability 📈

Systems can handle many users or tasks at the same time.

Used in microservices, messaging systems, and distributed architectures.

Handling Long-Running Tasks ⏳

Tasks like sending emails, video processing, report generation run in the background.

Example tools: message queues, background workers.

