When async tasks fail, exceptions occur in background threads.
CompletableFuture provides methods to handle these errors without crashing the application.

Common methods:

exceptionally()

handle()

whenComplete()

1️⃣ exceptionally() – Handle Exception and Return Default Value

Used when you want to catch an exception and return a fallback result.

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class ExceptionExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        CompletableFuture<Integer> future =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 10 / 0)
                .exceptionally(ex -> {
                    System.out.println("Error: " + ex.getMessage());
                    return 0;
                });

        System.out.println(future.join());
    }
}

Output:

Error: / by zero
0

✔ Handles exception
✔ Returns default value

2️⃣ handle() – Handle Both Success and Failure

handle() executes whether the task succeeds or fails.

CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 10 / 0)
        .handle((result, ex) -> {
            if (ex != null) {
                System.out.println("Exception occurred");
                return 0;
            }
            return result;
        })
        .thenAccept(System.out::println);

✔ Works for both success and error cases

3️⃣ whenComplete() – Execute After Completion

Runs after task completion, but does not change the result.

CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "Task completed")
        .whenComplete((result, ex) -> {
            if (ex == null) {
                System.out.println("Result: " + result);
            }
        });

✔ Used for logging or monitoring

📊 Quick Comparison
Method	Use Case
exceptionally()	Return fallback value
handle()	Handle success + error
whenComplete()	Post-processing/logging
