🎯 Best Interview Answer (45 seconds)

@Async, CompletableFuture, and Reactive Programming are three approaches for asynchronous processing in Java. @Async is a Spring Boot annotation used to execute methods in background threads and is suitable for simple tasks like sending emails. CompletableFuture is part of Java's concurrency API and supports advanced async workflows like chaining and combining multiple tasks. Reactive programming, implemented with libraries like Project Reactor, follows a non-blocking event-driven model and is designed for highly scalable systems handling thousands of concurrent requests.

✅ Important Interview Tip

Reactive programming is powerful, but it isn’t always the best choice. Use it only when the problem benefits from non-blocking, asynchronous streams.

1️⃣ Low Concurrency / Simple Applications

Small apps with few users or light traffic.

Overhead of reactive libraries (Reactor/WebFlux) may increase complexity unnecessarily.

Example:

A small internal admin dashboard serving 50 users a day doesn’t need reactive streams.

2️⃣ CPU-Bound Tasks

Reactive programming excels at I/O-bound tasks (DB calls, APIs, network I/O).

If the task is CPU-heavy, non-blocking won’t help because the event loop will be blocked anyway.

Example:

Image processing, encryption, video transcoding.

These tasks are better handled in parallel threads or Executors, not reactive streams.

3️⃣ Legacy or Blocking Libraries

If you rely on blocking libraries (JDBC, old HTTP clients), reactive programming won’t magically make them non-blocking.

Using reactive with blocking calls can degrade performance.

Solution: Use R2DBC for DB or WebClient (non-blocking HTTP).

4️⃣ Teams Not Familiar with Reactive Paradigm

Reactive programming has a steep learning curve.

If the team is not experienced, it can lead to bugs, maintenance headaches, and poor performance.

5️⃣ Simple CRUD APIs

If your system is just simple CRUD operations with minimal concurrency, Spring MVC + @Async or CompletableFuture is simpler and easier to maintain.

