Reactive programming is not ideal for CPU-bound tasks, small/simple apps, or systems that rely on blocking libraries.
It’s best suited for I/O-heavy, high-concurrency applications.
 For simple CRUD APIs or when the team is inexperienced with reactive paradigms, it’s better to use @Async or CompletableFuture.

 Reactive programming is a non-blocking, event-driven approach to handle streams of data asynchronously. In Java, it is mainly implemented using Project Reactor and Spring WebFlux. It is designed for high-concurrency, I/O-heavy applications.

 1️⃣ Key Concepts
 Concept	Explanation
 Publisher	Produces data asynchronously. (Mono or Flux)
 Subscriber	Consumes data emitted by the Publisher.
 Mono	Emits 0 or 1 item (single result).
 Flux	Emits 0 to N items (stream of results).
 Backpressure	Mechanism to handle data overflow when producer is faster than consumer.
 2️⃣ Mono Example (Single Result)
 import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

 public class MonoExample {
     public static void main(String[] args) {

         Mono<String> mono = Mono.just("Hello Reactive");

         mono.subscribe(System.out::println); // prints "Hello Reactive"
     }
 }

 Mono.just() → creates a reactive stream with one item

 subscribe() → consumes the data

 3️⃣ Flux Example (Multiple Items)
 import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;

 public class FluxExample {
     public static void main(String[] args) {

         Flux<String> flux = Flux.just("A", "B", "C");

         flux.subscribe(System.out::println); // prints A B C
     }
 }

 Useful for streaming multiple items (e.g., transaction lists).

 4️⃣ Chaining and Transforming

 Reactive streams allow functional chaining:

 Flux.just(1, 2, 3, 4)
     .map(i -> i * 2)
     .filter(i -> i > 4)
     .subscribe(System.out::println);

 Output:

 6
 8

 map() transforms data

 filter() filters items

 Everything is non-blocking

 5️⃣ WebFlux Example (Reactive REST API)
 @RestController
 public class UserController {

     @GetMapping("/users")
     public Flux<String> getUsers() {
         return Flux.just("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"); // reactive stream
     }
 }

 Spring WebFlux automatically streams results non-blockingly

 Handles thousands of concurrent requests without spawning threads per request

 6️⃣ Why Use Reactive Programming

 ✅ Handles high-concurrency, I/O-heavy workloads
 ✅ Non-blocking → better resource utilization
 ✅ Backpressure support → prevents system overload
 ✅ Ideal for microservices and streaming APIs

 7️⃣ When NOT to Use

 CPU-heavy tasks

 Small, simple applications

 Blocking legacy libraries (e.g., JDBC without R2DBC)

 Teams not familiar with reactive paradigms

 8️⃣ Real-World Banking Example

 Use case: Live transaction stream on dashboard

 Flux<Transaction> transactions = transactionService.streamTransactions(userId);

 transactions
     .filter(t -> t.getAmount() > 1000)
     .map(t -> t.getSummary())
     .subscribe(System.out::println);

 Streams all transactions non-blocking

 Filters and transforms data on-the-fly

 Can support millions of concurrent users

 🎯 30-Second Interview Answer

 Reactive programming in Java, implemented using Project Reactor and Spring WebFlux, is a non-blocking,
  event-driven approach to handle asynchronous data streams.
  Mono handles a single result, Flux handles multiple results.
  It is ideal for I/O-heavy, high-concurrency systems like live dashboards or streaming APIs.
  Unlike traditional threads, reactive systems use event loops, which improves scalability and resource efficiency.